{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when HaShem your Elohim grants you rest.</b> After you inherit the land you shall blot out the remembrance of Amalek, as it is written (I Samuel 15:3): ״והמתה מאיש ועד אשה מעולל ועד יונק משור ועד שה״ — 'and strike them from man to woman, from infant to suckling, from ox to sheep' — so that they should not say, 'this ox belonged to Amalek.' And further, from what the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded, that they would make themselves into beasts through the wisdom of sorcery and turn back into whatever species they wished; therefore it was necessary to say 'from ox to sheep, from camel to donkey'; and therefore the wisdom of the Torah prevailed over them, so that their wisdom would not avail them." "<b>From under the heavens.</b> That is, the Holy One, blessed be He, will blot out their power in heaven. And so Balaam prophesied concerning him (Numbers 24:20): ״ואחריתו עדי אובד״ — 'his latter end is even unto destruction' — until the Destroyer comes, this being the Holy One, blessed be He, of whom it is written (Exodus 17:14): ״כי מחה אמחה״ — 'for I will utterly blot out.' At first 'I will blot out' above, and in the end 'you shall blot out' below. לא תשכח — 'you shall not forget' — that is, it shall not be forgotten." "<b>And this pericah</b> began with remembrance and ended with forgetting, to indicate that their remembrance is destined to be forgotten from the world, and this is in the time of the Messiah. And even though the plain sense of the verse according to the words is that it shall not be forgotten, the allusion in it according to the accents (te'amim) is that a time will come when it shall be forgotten. And therefore you will find a paseq-revii combination on the word לא, and the word תשכח stands by itself, and there is a dagesh in the letter tav — that is to say, behold, days are coming when it shall be forgotten, when their remembrance perishes from the world. This is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 9:7–8): ״האויב תמו חרבות לנצח וערים נתשת אבד זכרם המה״ — 'the enemy are finished, ruins forever; their cities you have uprooted; their remembrance is perished' — and then the Name will be whole and the Throne will be whole, and this is what is written immediately after (ibid.): ״וה׳ לעולם ישב כונן למשפט כסאו״ — 'and HaShem sits forever; He has established His throne for judgment.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The phrase טרחא במלת לא refers to the cantillation accent serving as a prosodic break (paseq) after לא; translated as 'a paseq-revii combination on the word לא' to convey the te'amim phenomenon he cites. The dagesh in the tav of תשכח is morphological (after the conjunction), but he reads it here as a hermeneutic signal."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:12.941562585Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}